<Base title="Project" path="/project">
  <section class="project-intro">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="title">What is Music4Resilience?</h2>
      <p>Music4Resilience is <b>a project that trains and equips youth workers</b> to use music when supporting young people in difficult situations: young people with disabilities or mental health issues, from disadvantaged backgrounds, or in hospital. Youth practitioners are often the <b>first point of contact</b> for young people facing complex personal, social and emotional challenges, yet many lack practical, evidence-based tools. Music4Resilience develops and implements <b>inclusive music workshops</b> across youth centres and supporting organisations in Luxembourg.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="project-body">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h4>Why?</h4>
      <p>Three out of four youth workers report a <b>significant rise in mental health-related questions</b> from young people, while most have had little structured training in this area. At the same time, music is a core part of young people's lives: it offers <b>emotional support</b> and fosters <b>self-expression, identity and belonging</b>.</p>
      <h4>What?</h4>
      <p>The heart of the project is the <b>Music4Resilience community-based lab</b>: a series of four music-informed workshops that offer a space to explore music and musical resources, so that youth workers can engage vulnerable young people through music and promote <b>well-being, social inclusion and employability</b>.</p>
      <h4>How?</h4>
      <p>Each workshop is a <b>3-hour, face-to-face, experiential session</b> grounded in mental health and socio-emotional skills methodologies. It combines simple language and visual aids with <b>listening, songwriting, accessible instruments and easy-to-use music technology</b> (e.g., launchpads, Artiphon Orba &amp; Chorda, Roland Aira beat machines). The voices of participants guide the design and content of the workshops.</p>
      <h4>Impact</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>We aim to <b>strengthen the confidence and capacity</b> of youth practitioners to use music in their daily work with young people.</li>
        <li>We aim to provide <b>accessible, culturally adaptable music resources</b> that are practical and transferable to different youth settings.</li>
        <li>We aim to support youth workers <b>not only as service providers, but also as individuals</b>, by embedding reflective music practices and self-care in the training.</li>
        <li>We aim to contribute to the <b>recognition of youth work</b> as a profession that safeguards young people's well-being and future.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>Evaluation</h4>
      <p>The project is evaluated throughout, using <b>pre/post questionnaires</b> (well-being, self-confidence, emotional regulation, communication), <b>feedback forms</b> after each workshop, and <b>self-assessment of professional competence</b> in mental health literacy, emotional support and music methodologies.</p>
      <h4>Final Aim</h4>
      <p>Workshop materials will remain <b>available online</b> after the project and feed into IFEN training, courses offered by Luxembourgish associations, and the <b>inclusive music education</b> strand of the University of Luxembourg Bachelor in Music Education.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="workshops" id="workshops">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The workshops</h2>
      <p class="intro">Four in-person workshops, <b>January to April 2027</b>. Each includes an introduction, a detailed programme outline, guidelines for implementation (group size, timing, roles, ethics, logistics), and tools for evaluation and self-reflection.</p>
      <div class="ws-grid">
        <div class="ws"><p class="num">1</p><h3>Music and Movement</h3><p>Emotion awareness, labelling and regulation.</p></div>
        <div class="ws"><p class="num">2</p><h3>Musical Care</h3><p>Communication (listening, presenting, assertiveness) and self-regulation (self-confidence, agency, self-efficacy).</p></div>
        <div class="ws"><p class="num">3</p><h3>Inclusive Music Making</h3><p>Planning and problem-solving: decision making, navigating resources, organisation.</p></div>
        <div class="ws"><p class="num">4</p><h3>Music, Technology and Disability</h3><p>Creativity and collaboration with accessible music technologies.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <p class="funded wrap">Music4Resilience is a project of the <a class="rust" href="https://www.uni.lu/fhse-en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Education and Social Work</a> at the University of Luxembourg, supported by the <a class="blue" href="https://www.loschfondation.lu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Losch Fondation</a></p>
</Base>
